# AgentDecompile — Cursor instructions

Read [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md) for repository operations and safety,
[docs/USER_INTENT.md](docs/USER_INTENT.md) for the current product contract,
[STRATEGY.md](STRATEGY.md) for recovery sequencing and proof, and
[METHODOLOGY.md](METHODOLOGY.md) for durable engineering principles. These are
the shared authority; this file must not create a competing product definition
or copy its detailed pipeline.

AgentDecompile is a higher-level reverse-engineering workbench built around
Ghidra. It joins related binaries, aims to recover C/C++ that compiles to match
the actual target, and makes cross-build identity, ABI, events and runtime
evidence usable for supported analysis, WASM/graphics, Lua, shim/DLL, hook/cave
and memory workflows. Do not reduce it to an MCP wrapper or decompiler pane.
Do not treat runtime success, model output, copied bytes or partial function
matches as source or whole-image proof.

For implementation, derive command names, options, defaults, aliases and UI
controls from the authoritative parser/schema/provider that owns the behavior.
Do not grow parallel hand-maintained spelling lists, capability catalogs,
workflow defaults or acceptance rules in each frontend. Shared interfaces must
preserve the same selected binary, saved evidence, actual jobs and claim status.

Python under `src/` is the active implementation. Inspect the owning module and
its consumers before editing; preserve public behavior and user changes. Verify
the behavior actually affected, and report source, live-runtime, and proof
evidence separately. During the current recovery mission, the stricter
no-filesystem-tests/builds and 8080-only operating rules in `AGENTS.md` apply.

